  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nWeak and dry clipper system will push across southern Canada\ntoday. Ahead of this front will be moist southerly flow, which\nwill then switch to a warm and dry westerly flow. Areas of dense\nfog will be found in this southerly flow this morning, mainly \nin southwest and south central portions. Expect this to linger\nmost of the morning or until the mentioned clipper system can\nswitch winds to a dry westerly flow. Thus these mentioned areas\nnow have a Dense Fog Advisory through the morning. Meanwhile, \nlingering fog in the northwest continues to diminish and become \nmore patchy. Issued a short SPS to account for this early this \nmorning. Otherwise look for warming temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions today once winds become westerly with highs in the\nmid 40s to mid 50s. Dry conditions look to continue through\ntonight with more mild lows in the mid 20s to mid 30s. Some fog\ncould return, although may generally be confined to the\nsoutheast where lighter winds are forecast. Zonal to northwest\nflow aloft, with a quick moving clipper followed by ridging at \nthe surface, will bring a mainly dry and warm weekend. Look for \nhighs in the mid 50s to mid 60s each day, with mild lows in the \n30s. Another dry clipper system could bring some breezy westerly\nwinds on Saturday, although these should remain below advisory \ncriteria with the main clipper staying to the north. Steady\nsoutherly winds then look to return for Sunday.\n\nAbove normal temperatures and mainly dry conditions remain in\nthe forecast for early to mid next week. Broad ridging to our\nwest cold allow for some weak impulses at times in northwest\nflow across the region. Most of these have limited moisture to\nwork with and the current forecast only has slight pops on\nTuesday. Near to above normal temperatures in the mid 40s to \nmid 50s are forecast each day, with lows also mild and generally\nin the 30s. Any disturbances that move through could bring some\nbreezy winds at times, with some breezy westerly winds forecast\nfor Monday. Clusters have the western ridge moving through the\nare later next week. This will maintain the above normal\ntemperatures along with mainly dry conditions.",
